 "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23418728/freeing-an-array-of-character-pointers-string-literals",
 "body_html": "<p>I'm trying to figure out how to free the memory for an array of character pointers (string literals), but I can't quite get the syntax. This is how I'm declaring and initializing the arrays.</p>\n\n<pre><code>char * words[] = { \"THESE\", \"ARE\", \"SOME\", \"WORDS\" };\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>I've tried doing this...</p>\n\n<pre><code>free(words);\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>And this...</p>\n\n<pre><code>for(i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {\n    free(words[i]);\n}\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>But the first one causes some sort of invalid pointer error with glibc, and the second one causes a segmentation fault.</p>\n\n<p>So what's the right way to free this memory?</p>\n",
 "passes": [
  "main"
 ],
 "data_source": "so_main_cached (2026-10-05 collection)",
 "in_main_retained_480": true,
 "in_main_nonNA_coded_346": true,
